<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of Capistrano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1386">1386</a>–<a href="/years/1456">1456</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Saint John of Capistrano, Giovanni da Capestrano</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of Capistrano was an Italian Franciscan friar and preacher who campaigned across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> and for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In 1456 he raised a crusading army of peasants and townsmen that, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>'s forces, broke the Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. He died of plague soon afterwards and was canonised in 1690.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-of-capistrano.jpg" alt="John of Capistrano" width="320" height="705" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:82Johannes_von_Capestrano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was born on 24 June 1386 and was named after the village of Capestrano in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzi</a>; his father had come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with the Angevin court of Louis I of Anjou. He studied law at the University of Perugia, and in 1412 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> appointed him governor of Perugia. In 1416, sent as ambassador to broker peace in a war between Perugia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-family">House of Malatesta</a>, he was thrown into prison, and began to study theology there. On his release he decided to become a Franciscan, citing a dream of Saint Francis; he had married before the war but asserted that the marriage was never consummated. He entered the Order at Perugia on 4 October 1416.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1420 John preached in many cities, and he was ordained in 1425. Unlike most Italian preachers of repentance, he was effective in northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> he preached to a crowd of 126,000. With Bernardine of Siena he worked to reform the Franciscan Order, and in 1429 both were acquitted by a commission of cardinals of charges of heresy raised over their devotion to the Holy Name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. In 1451 he went as apostolic nuncio to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and as legate or inquisitor he persecuted the last Fraticelli of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, the Jesuati of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, Crypto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>.</p><p>John preached extensively against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and Silesia and was called the scourge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrews</a>. Between 1451 and 1453 his sermons persuaded many southern German regions to expel their entire Jewish populations. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a>, after an accusation that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had desecrated the communion bread, he supervised the torture of Jewish prisoners until they confessed; forty were burned alive, a rabbi hanged himself and the rest were expelled. Scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and of Judaism have judged him differently: a 2005 encyclopedia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> said he was a forerunner of modern antisemitism in seeking the social isolation of Jews.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-iii">Pope Callixtus III</a> sent John, then aged 70, to preach a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Meeting little response in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, he concentrated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and by July 1456 had raised a large but poorly trained force of peasants and landlords. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> he advanced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, where together they had gathered about 40,000 to 50,000 troops. After a fierce battle the Ottomans withdrew and the siege was lifted, and John was called the Soldier Priest. He died of plague on 23 October 1456 at Ilok. His canonisation was marked in 1690 by Pope Alexander VIII. He is the patron saint of military chaplains and jurists.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John of Capistrano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John Hunyadi, voivode of Transylvania and later governor of the kingdom, won victories in the Long Campaign of 1443–1444 and lost the Second Battle of Kosovo in 1448, but in July 1456 broke the Ottoman siege of Belgrade with the preacher <b>John of Capistrano</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Capestrano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Ilok. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.27,13.77&amp;year=1431" aria-label="Capestrano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4884 660 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John of Capistrano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5334 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5334 930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.27,13.77&amp;year=1431">What was here in 1431? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
